PANEL 6 (Large, road encounter – 20% of image height): A dusty road through a rocky pass. Alucard rides toward the camera. Another figure approaches from the opposite direction – a woman on a grey horse, wearing the silver holy symbol of the Church of Light. She is a demon hunter, though Alucard does not recognize her. She holds up a hand, stopping him. Her expression is wary but not hostile. Speech bubble from the woman (cautious): "Alucard. I've heard of you." Alucard's bubble (flat): "Most people have." Woman: "The Monastery says you're dangerous. That the corruption in your arm makes you unpredictable." Alucard (after a pause): "The Monastery says a lot of things. Did they also tell you I've killed more demons in the last five years than any three of their hunters combined?" The woman is silent. Then she nods, slowly, and moves aside to let him pass. Caption at bottom of panel: "Occasionally, he would encounter other demon hunters on the road – agents of the Church, mostly. Some recognized his name. Some looked at him with suspicion. None of them challenged him. None of them asked about the corruption. They simply nodded, exchanged information about demon movements, and went their separate ways."

PANEL 7 (Medium, memory fragment – 10% of image height): A ghostly, semi-transparent memory panel – Tigreal's silhouette, standing in the Monastery courtyard, watching Alucard train. The memory is sepia-toned, bleeding at the edges like an old photograph. Alucard's current self (opaque) rides through the foreground, the memory behind him. Caption: "Alucard preferred it that way."

PANEL 8 (Small, campfire reflection – 8% of image height): Night. Alucard sits by a small campfire, his horse tied nearby. He holds his mother's sketch in one hand and his father's medal in the other. His face is soft for a moment – vulnerable. Then he puts them away, stands, and picks up his sword. Caption: "The weight of reputation was nothing compared to the weight of his name. Alucard. Dracula spelled backward. The life-drinker, the blood-letter, the monster who hunts monsters. He did not choose the name. It was given to him. He does not blame anyone for their ignorance. He does not blame anyone for anything, really, except perhaps the men who sent his father to die and then called him a coward for not surviving."

PANEL 9 (Bottom, wide, cinematic – 12% of image height): Alucard standing on a ridge at dawn, overlooking a valley where a demonic incursion is visible as a dark stain on the land. His sword is drawn, planted before him. His silhouette is sharp against the rising sun. The gauntlet's blue glow is the only color in the grey landscape. Caption in bold handwritten font across the bottom: "The demons will not wait while he cares about reputation. The Abyss will not pause because some people call him a monster. So he hunts. He kills. He moves on. That is all he knows. That is all he needs to know." Final line, italic: "The road goes on. The hunt never ends."

