PANEL 2 (Medium, face close-up – 12% of image height): Close-up on Alucard's face, side-lit by the rising sun. His icy blue eyes are looking into the distance, not at anything specific. His expression is not sad – it is accepting. There is a small, almost imperceptible softening around his mouth. Caption below: "And yet, he continues to fight. This is the paradox at the heart of Alucard's existence. He is not an optimist – he has never been an optimist, not even as a child. He knows that the Abyss will never truly be defeated, that the demons will always find new ways to slip through, that the corruption in his arm will eventually consume him."

PANEL 3 (Small, gesture – 8% of image height): A close-up on Alucard's gauntleted right hand, resting on the pommel of his sword. The runes glow faintly. His fingers are relaxed, not clenched. He is not holding on – he is simply touching. Caption: "But he also knows that every demon he kills is a demon that will not kill someone else. Every incursion he stops is a village that will survive to see another sunrise. Every battle he wins is a small, temporary victory against the darkness – and sometimes, small victories are the only kind that matter."

PANEL 4 (Large, campfire reflection – 20% of image height): Night. Alucard sits by a small campfire in a rocky hollow. The firelight flickers on his face, casting dancing shadows. He is not looking at the fire – he is looking at his mother's sketch and his father's medal, laid out on a flat stone beside him. His horse Ember lies nearby, asleep. The gauntlet's blue glow mixes with the orange firelight. Caption at top of panel: "He fights because he cannot stop. He fights because the alternative – sitting still, doing nothing, letting the darkness spread – is unacceptable. He fights because his father taught him that duty is not about reward, is not about recognition, is not about the history books. Duty is simply doing what needs to be done, because it needs to be done, and because someone has to do it."

PANEL 5 (Medium, standing watch – 12% of image height): Alucard standing at the entrance of a ruined building, looking out into the darkness. His sword is drawn, held low. His posture is relaxed but ready. The moon is visible through a broken roof. He is waiting – for what, the viewer does not know. Caption below: "That, more than any technique or weapon, is Alucard's greatest strength. He does not fight for glory. He does not fight for recognition. He fights because someone must."

PANEL 6 (Small, after a battle – 8% of image height): A battlefield after the fighting is done. Alucard stands among the bodies of demons (dissolving into ash) and a few fallen soldiers (covered with cloaks, respectful). He is wiping his sword clean with a rag. His face is blank – no triumph, no grief. Just exhaustion. Caption: "He has thought about what he would do if the war ever ended. If the Abyss were sealed, if the demons were destroyed, if the corruption in his arm were somehow cleansed. What would be left for him? He does not have an answer."

PANEL 7 (Medium, walking away – 10% of image height): A long shot of Alucard walking away from the battlefield, his back to the camera. The sun is rising behind him, casting his shadow long ahead. His greatsword is slung across his back. His horse follows behind, riderless for now. The landscape is empty. Caption: "The war has been going on for so long that he cannot imagine a world without it. The hunt has become his identity, his reason for existing. Without it, he would be nothing – just a man with a cursed arm and a haunted past, drifting through a world that has no place for him."

PANEL 8 (Bottom, wide, cinematic – 15% of image height): Alucard sitting on a rocky outcrop at sunset, overlooking a valley where a small village's lights are just beginning to flicker on. Smoke rises from chimneys. People are going about their lives, unaware of the hunter watching over them from the dark. Alucard's face is in profile, the setting sun behind him. The gauntlet's blue glow is a small, cold light against the warm sky. Caption in bold handwritten font across the bottom: "So he does not think about the end of the war. He focuses on the next battle, the next demon, the next village that needs protection. One step at a time. One kill at a time. One day at a time. It is not a philosophy that would satisfy a scholar or a priest. But it is enough for Alucard." Final line, italic: "The hunt continues. It always continues."

