FINAL NOTE PROMPT – 9:16 VERTICAL IMAGE
Create a 9:16 vertical masterwork that depicts an ancient, weathered parchment – the final testament of Alucard, the Demon Hunter. The document is presented as if found in an old chest, a forgotten corner of a library, or pressed between the pages of a journal. The image is hyperrealistic 8K, with cinematic lighting (soft, diffused, like late afternoon sunlight through a dusty window), anamorphic lens feel, sharp focus on the paper texture and ink, subsurface scattering for the parchment fibers, visible paper grain, ink pooling, micro-details of tears and stains, film grain, ARRI Alexa color grading, 85mm f/1.8 equivalent depth of field.
COMPOSITION & LAYOUT:
The parchment fills almost the entire frame, slightly angled (rotated a few degrees, as if lying on a wooden desk). The edges are irregular – torn, singed, water-stained. The paper is thick, cream-colored but darkened to a warm sepia-brown at the edges. Fold lines cross the document (it has been folded into thirds, then opened). A wax seal (the user's logo) is affixed to the lower right corner, with a crimson ribbon wrapped around the document and tied through the seal. A small ink wash portrait of Alucard (the evolved version, peaceful, sitting on his stone throne) is painted in the upper left corner, serving as a visual signature. The rest of the document is filled with handwritten text in dark brown-black ink, with visible variations in pen pressure, ink splatters, and crossed-out corrections.
CONTENT OF THE PARCHMENT:
The text is written in a neat but personal cursive, with some words in bold calligraphy. The language is formal yet intimate, as if Alucard wrote this for himself, or for whoever might find it after he is gone.
Heading (top center, large brushed calligraphy, Sosho influence):
"The Testament of Alucard, Son of Arton"
Main Text (in handwritten script, with paragraph breaks):
"I write this not for the empire, which has forgotten my father's name. I write this not for the Church, which would burn these pages if it knew their contents. I write this for the one who comes after – the hunter who will walk the same dark path, who will carry a weight not of their choosing, who will wonder if the fight is worth the cost.
It is worth it. Every scar, every sleepless night, every moment the corruption whispers in your ear – it is worth it. Because there are villages that still stand because you stood before them. There are children who sleep soundly because you woke to the howl of demons. There is a world that does not know your name, but would not exist without you.
My father taught me that duty is not about reward. It is about doing what needs to be done because someone has to do it. I have tried to live by that code. I have failed, at times. I have been cruel when I should have been kind. I have been distant when I should have reached out. But I have never stopped fighting.
The gauntlet is breaking. The corruption spreads. I do not know how much time I have left. Perhaps I will become what I hunt. Perhaps someone will have to put me down. If that day comes, know this: I am not afraid. I have made peace with the darkness inside me.
To the hunter who reads this – do not let the whispers win. You are not alone. You are not a monster. You are carrying a burden that would crush anyone else, and you are still standing. That is enough.
May the light guide you. And when the light fails, may you find your own way forward.
— Alucard"
Signature (bottom center, large, flourishing, with a small sketch of a wolf's head next to it):
"Alucard – The Demon Hunter"
Date (below signature, smaller script):
"The 27th year of the Moniyan Empire, on the edge of the Barren Lands. Dawn."
